  "worked_solution": "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(5,4)$, can be found by computing $\u2207f(5,4).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(5,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\text{\u00a0}i+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\text{\u00a0}j \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\text{\u00a0}i-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\text{\u00a0}j \\\\ \u2207f(5,4) & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{(5)^{2}-(4)^{2}}}\\text{\u00a0}i-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{(5)^{2}-(4)^{2}}}\\text{\u00a0}j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{9}}i-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{9}}j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}i-\\frac{4}{3}j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\u2207f(5,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2207f(5,4)}{\u2016\u2207f(5,4)\u2016} & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{3}i-\\frac{4}{3}j}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{5}{3})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{3}i-\\frac{4}{3}j}{\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{41}(5i-4j)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
